2|0@atheism^1438614491#ctpxxzj:massivelight$:I've always thought of it like there are many ways to explain the natural world around us .. there was a time when religion had the explanations, though dubious, and people accepted it mostly because of the 'argument from ignorance fallacy'. Fast forward to a time later when these fallacies in religious teachings were so replete, that some members of the human race went about observing their known (and unknown) surroundings through experiments, observing and recording results, tests, and building models. Eventually, this [scientific method] became the primary tool to understand and explain the world around us. It is the best tool we have at the moment. It allows for change, understanding of the evidence, no biases, and no authority in which it's students must obey. There have been misfindings through science which is admittedly welcome because it excites the community to learn more, understand more, and solve mysteries. Religion, as far as I can see, is sticky to change and doesn't account for verifiable evidence. It is based merely on faith. This sort of thinking teaches people to become lazy and apathetic to the truth. It inhibits curiosity, fascination, and the ability to think critically. Is science a religion? No. Is religion a science? Absolutely not. They are ways to understand the world around us. They are not the only ways, but it's what we have. And the scientific method has made all of our lives better. In fact, the 'comfort' religion gives or in my opinion 'false promises' is not unique to religion. Comfort can be attained through science as well. Carl sagan, NDT, Sam harris, and others have spoken on this topic. TL;DR - no, science is not a religion 
