1|0@atheism^1438627006#ctq64m0:LeannaBard$:Here is the definition of religion: "the belief in and worship of a superhuman controlling power, especially a personal God or gods." It may also be extended to "a set of beliefs and practices concerning the afterlife." Here is the definition of science: "the intellectual and practical activity encompassing the systematic study of the structure and behavior of the physical and natural world through observation and experiment." Science is not a belief in or worship of a superhuman controlling deity, and it is not concerned with the afterlife. Nothing about science makes it fit the definition of religion. If you choose to define religion as "any set of rules, practices, or anything one is emphatic about," then nearly everything becomes a religion and it reduces the term to being nearly useless.
0|0@atheism^1438624179#ctq46r7:commandrix$:With science, any experiment that can be independently repeated by other researchers with the same, or at least very similar, results is considered valid. Any experiment that can't reproduce the same results under the exact same conditions under the supervision of an unbiased independent researcher is considered invalid. Religion tends to ignore and/or prosecute the "independent researchers" who can't come up with the same results and, therefore, call the validity of the original results into question.
1|0@atheism^1438622891#ctq3aul:JimDixon$:Religions believe: 1. There is a god (or gods). 2. God wants us to do certain things. 3. God has revealed what he wants us to do by speaking to various human prophets. 4. The words of those prophets have been passed down to us in infallible holy texts. 5. There is an organization whose purpose is to interpret those holy texts and promote belief in them. 6. That organization is also certainly right and we should trust them and be obedient to them. Science has none of those things.
1|0@atheism^1438672175#ctqv67b:KurtWagnerX$:Science is arguably a philosophical position that testing a predictive hypothesis and be willing to reject it if it consistently fails to match predictions is the most productive way to understand the universe. There may be an enthusiastic adherence to the process, but if the process of testing hypotheses were to suddenly start failing, then hopefully by the very premise of this process we would reject the process itself
